00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.970
we got about it in Google.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.410
I will provide
some explanations,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:03.450
what Cloud Spanner is.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.700
I also go down to some
architectural level

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.890
and talk about the
technologies and the hardware

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.610
that we use to actually
make that possible, what we

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.660
can achieve with Cloud Spanner.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.460
And at the end, I'm
going to do some coding

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.440
and show you a demo.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.960
Now, I'm going to talk
about Cloud Spanner

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.450
and look at the history of it.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.010
We have to go back
around 12 years to 2005.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.020
Now, Google at that
point has been around

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.400
for a couple of years, and our
business was growing massively.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.810
So we had our AdWords system
on the back of a sharded MySQL.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:40.950
System.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.730
Now, this MySQL system was
sharded on the customer ID,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:49.080
and we had to reshard
the system several times.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.880
The last time we
resharded the system

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.140
it took actually several years.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.670
And this was not a solution
that we could go forward.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.850
With the way that
we were growing,

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.950
this was not a
sustainable solution.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:08.139
So we needed something
that can scale to the point

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:10.590
that we needed it.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:14.890
So we needed something
that horizontally scales.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.110
We had a lot of customers.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.000
We were growing quickly.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.470
We needed a system which
is still strong consistent

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.860
and has the relational
database attributes

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.040
like the asset transactions.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.342
So we were dealing with money.

00:02:31.342 --> 00:02:32.550
We were dealing with budgets.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.540
We had customers.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.440
We had partners.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.970
We had agencies.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.700
So eventual consistency
was not an option for us.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.740
Now, also, every minute the
system was down, we and also

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.210
our customers were losing money.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.720
So we couldn't accept any
downtime for the system.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.760
We need a system where
we can make changes,

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.830
where we can make updates, where
we can make security patches

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.400
without any incurred downtime.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.820
So prior to Spanner,
there was no system

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.980
that could provide
all these qualities,

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.850
and that's the reason
we built it ourselves.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.700
Now, if we look at
Cloud Spanner compared

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:18.520
to what we have internally,
you get the same system,

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.280
the same performance,
the same consistency

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.190
as the internal Spanner
that we are using.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:27.160
So its the same site
reliability engineers

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.230
that are watching
our systems that

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.110
also watching your systems.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:36.190
Now, as mentioned already,
we needed asset transactions.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.230
We needed global consistency.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:43.180
We actually have the highest
standard of the consistency.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.060
Our consistency model
is external [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:52.020
Now, one important
part was also that we

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.110
wanted to tap into the
knowledge and the experience

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.040
of our developers.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.670
We didn't want to put
a system in their hands

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.070
which is completely
different to what they

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.660
have been dealing with all their
developer time or developer

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.530
life.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.540
So for us, it was
really important

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.660
that certain
semantics are the same

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:16.350
as you would think of them in a
traditional relational database

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.450
system.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.290
On top of this, we added things
like automatic and synchronous

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.700
replication so we can
span multiple regions

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:30.210
and we can actually span
globally in Spanner.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.730
And for a regional
system, that means

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:37.120
that we provide an SLA of
four nines of availability.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.740
So this is about four minutes
and 38 seconds of downtime

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.000
that we allow per month.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.850
Now, for our
multiregional setups,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.100
where you span your database
over multiple regions--

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.980
and I will talk a little
bit about what that means--

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.920
we actually provide an SLA with
five nines of availability,

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.250
which boils down
to about 26 seconds

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.580
of allowed downtime per month.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.450
Another really
important aspect for us

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:09.330
when we were building Cloud
Spanner was to make it open,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:14.980
to enable customers and
developers to adopt it easily.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.850
So when we started
and when we were

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.120
working with our
early-access customers,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.350
we were focusing on making it
as easy as possible to adopt it.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.760
So some of these things, we
were using standard SQL NZ 2011.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.210
We actually use this across
most of our products, database

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.550
products, within Google.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.800
We added things like
enterprise features,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:42.330
like encryption, audit locking,
identity access management,

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:43.860
things like that.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:48.210
We spent quite some time
and invested some resources

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.180
in building our client
libraries in major languages,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.760
and there are more and more
of these language libraries

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.810
coming up with how to
connect automatically

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.580
to the Cloud Spanner.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.280
And currently in a
read-only version,

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.390
we also have a GDPC driver
for Cloud Spanner, where

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.480
you can connect your most
favorite like the iTools

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.200
directly to Spanner
and use them.

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:19.510
One of the things that we
always get asked or compared to

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:22.160
is, on the one hand, the
traditional relational database

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.030
system, and on the
other hand the NoSQL,

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.030
horizontally-scalable
data systems.

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:34.100
And as you see here, we put
up a small matrix of features

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:37.070
that we are comparing here.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.980
So if you look at
a traditional RDBMS

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:43.100
and you outscale this
system, usually what

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:48.200
are you going to do is you build
a master replica system where

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.580
you have your master--

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:50.840
like a MySQL master--

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.060
and then you have a
failover instance,

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.970
and you might have some
read-through replicas

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:58.580
to free up your master
of read capacity

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.370
to have more write
capacity on your master.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:05.120
Now, if you want to do any
kind of maintenance on this,

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.120
usually what you have to do is
to failover to your failover

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.990
instance if you do maintenance
on your master-- for instance,

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:15.340
security patches or an
update of the version.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.200
Now, that incurs downtime
and loses you money,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.930
and it's quite some operational
risk that you incur there.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:27.400
On the other hand, if you look
at like the NoSQL systems,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:34.750
if you basically [INAUDIBLE]
traditional RDBMS,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.540
many went through
NoSQL, and we're

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:41.350
looking at things like Cassandra
or other database systems that

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.390
are out there in
the NoSQL space.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.660
But what that meant
is, basically, yes, you

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.720
do have tunable
consistency, but most

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.390
of the times what we actually
do to get consistency,

00:07:52.390 --> 00:07:56.530
what still is required
in many businesses,

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.050
is that you build this
consistency and transaction

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.680
logic in your application layer.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.130
So you pull up this logic
into your application layer,

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:07.420
and that makes it quite complex
and difficult and risky.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.790
So with Cloud
Spanner, you basically

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:13.960
can move this logic
back into the database,

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.030
and you still have
these qualities

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:19.090
of a traditional database
system of asset transactions

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:22.060
with the scalability
of a NoSQL system.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:28.490
So a typical workload that
we are seeing, especially

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:31.360
with early-access
program members,

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.970
can be put in like
four categories.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:40.120
The first one is, if you have
a transactional system, things

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.510
like inventory management
or financial transactions

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.400
or something like
that, and you basically

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:48.100
share outgrow the size what
your traditional database

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:49.780
system can handle.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:52.990
Then, that is where
Spanner can come in.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:55.720
You still have the
need of transactions,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.300
but your traditional
systems can't handle

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.190
the size of the database.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.340
And Spanner can do this for you.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.710
With the horizontal scalability,
we can also scale the data size

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.103
or the database size. .

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:15.100
One of the things that we get
asked often is also, well,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.230
is Cloud Spanner only
for big databases?

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:18.850
And I have to say no.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.120
Even if you have small databases
which are a couple of gigabytes

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.110
big but you have a lot of
traffic on this database

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:28.570
and you outscale your
solutions in terms of read

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.940
and write transactions
that you can run,

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.520
that is another use
case for Cloud Spanner

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:37.350
if you need to scale, read,
and write transactions.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.530
Another thing is if you have
mission critical applications.

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:44.380
So you really need a database
system that is available

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:46.540
four nines or five
nines, and you just

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.910
can make any sacrifice on this.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:50.840
Again, that is something
where Cloud Spanner

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:52.180
comes into the plane.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.260
The way that we architect it
enables this high availability

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.900
and disaster recovery, and it
enables you to basically build

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:02.150
a global data plane
where you can have,

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:06.470
for instance, if you think of
supply chain management system,

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:09.370
and you want to really know
like what items are still

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.010
in your storage and
which items you have sold

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:15.350
and things like that, you want
to be as accurate as possible.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.520
And you want to have
a view on your data

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.890
as recent as possible.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:24.940
And the fourth is if you
think of systems where you

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.800
traditionally ETL data around.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.960
So for instance,
you have a user base

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:32.560
in a MySQL, Postgres
database and then

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.590
you have some logged data or
some audit data in a Cassandra

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:38.590
cluster.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:41.020
And then, at the
end of the day, you

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:46.030
ETL that into another system
and do some analytics on this.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:48.250
Now, this is again also a
use case for Cloud Spanner.

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:52.000
Since we can scale on read and
write as well as on storage,

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.740
you can combine and have these
data co-located in one database

00:10:56.740 --> 00:10:59.230
and do all the analytics there.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:06.060
So to talk a bit about more
of the architecture of Cloud

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:12.200
Spanner, I want to synchronize
on the terminology, so to say.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:15.230
And everything in Cloud Spanner
starts with an instance.

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:16.910
So the instance
is basically where

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:19.670
you would define
how many nodes you

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:22.310
want to have for your
instance, so what's

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:25.610
the horizontal scale
of your instance

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.830
and the capacity of storage.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:32.610
Now, in this instance, you can
create a bunch of databases.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:37.190
Currently, the limit is set
to 100 databases per instance.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:42.010
And then, in these databases,
you can create tables.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:47.130
Currently, per database, you
can create about 2,048 tables.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:50.500
Now, this is basically
what you see as a developer

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:52.140
and as a user.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.170
To enable us to
distribute your data

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:58.770
and to distribute and
scale on reads and writes,

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.850
we have another
concept underneath,

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:05.760
which is the internal
concept, which we call split.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:06.750
Now, what is a split?

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.940
A spit is basically if
you look at as a table

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:14.580
and you have your primary
key, we divide this table.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.940
And we divide that
based lexicographically

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.546
on the primary key,
or you could say also

00:12:21.546 --> 00:12:24.480
alphanumeric numeric
ordering or alphabetical

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:25.760
ordering basically.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.920
And we basically use
ranges and assign

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.500
these splits to work on nodes.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.710
Now, how does that look like?

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:37.980
So if you look at an instance
view of a Spanner instance,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:40.920
you have in a regional
instance a distribution

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.640
over three zones.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:46.410
So we have our compute on
the top as you can see.

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:49.880
One Spanner node means you have
one compute node in each zone

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:52.460
for a regional instance.

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:55.560
So for regional, that means
you have three compute nodes

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.210
in three zones.

00:12:57.210 --> 00:13:02.400
And then, you have storage
in all of those that

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:06.140
can be used by these nodes.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:09.680
So now if you want to
distribute the reads and writes,

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:13.760
you basically take these
splits and assign these splits

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.930
to a set of nodes across
these three zones.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:25.040
But, I mentioned earlier that
we are globally consistent.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.970
So one of the criteria here
that is really, really important

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.130
is that there's never more
than one group of nodes

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.890
responsible for a
split, and we actually

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:39.290
need one member
in this group who

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:43.280
is the one, the
leader of this group.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:47.570
And what we are using to do
this is, on the one hand, Paxos.

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:49.670
So the Paxos algorithm
basically allows

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:55.050
us to vote for a leader
with a majority voting.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.140
And we need another
thing to make sure

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.960
that we have exactly one Paxos
[INAUDIBLE] and exactly one

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:04.590
leader for any split.

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:07.140
And what we use there
is something that we

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:08.790
invented and called TrueTime.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:10.860
Now, what is TrueTime?

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:14.820
If you synchronize your
watches with your neighbor

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:18.150
and you go away and you
come back together in a week

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.150
and you look at your watches,
it's very highly likely

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:23.660
that your watches have drifted.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.860
So they drift because
of cosmic rays.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:29.270
They drift because of
different temperatures

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:30.156
that you have been.

00:14:30.156 --> 00:14:34.840
There are many factors that
let your watches drift.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:38.260
Now, to ensure for us that
we have exactly one leader,

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.710
we basically need something that
is close to a global wall clock

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.400
time.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:47.160
And I mentioned Spanner is
distributed across the globe,

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.820
so we need something where
we can synchronize time

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:52.300
across the globe.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:55.390
Now, it's not possible as I just
mentioned to just use a watch,

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:58.300
synchronize it in one place, and
goes somewhere and distribute

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.490
these watches to all
our data centers.

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:03.400
They will drift away and they
are not synchronized anymore,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.320
and then basically
what could happen

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:08.590
is that you have two leaders
for the same split, which

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:12.380
means your data can
become inconsistent.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.920
So what we needed is,
basically, we needed something

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:18.340
to quantify the drift that
we have between these data

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.350
centers, and we
needed a technology

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:24.970
to synchronize all these
watches in our data center.

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:27.850
So what TrueTime
does, actually it

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:30.310
doesn't give you
a specific time.

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:33.130
TrueTime gives you an interval.

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:36.400
So you have a lower
bound and an upper bound.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:40.900
Now, the lower bound of
your TrueTime timestamps

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.740
is a timestamp where you
know that this timestamp has

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:47.610
passed all over the world.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:51.050
Through this timestamp that
you get as a lower bound has

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:54.020
passed, you know that for sure.

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:56.480
Now, the upper
bound that you get

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:01.070
is a timestamp that has
passed nowhere in the world,

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:05.260
and you can be sure
that this is the case.

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:08.250
Now, we use this to
select the leaders

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:12.570
and make sure that we have
exactly one leader and one

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:14.070
Paxos group for a split.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:19.580
And by that, we can ensure that
we have global consistency.

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.390
Now, how does it
look in hardware?

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:28.030
In all our data centers, we have
atomic clocks and GPS receivers

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.170
or time clocks, so to say.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:35.650
And we use GPS to synchronize
the clocks around the globe.

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:38.130
And then, we have atomic
clocks, which we synchronize

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:41.020
with these GPS time masters.

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:45.550
And every time we ask for a
TrueTime timestamp or interval,

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:49.420
we use a subset of
our time masters

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:51.811
that are available
in our data center.

00:16:51.811 --> 00:16:52.810
And then we get it back.

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:54.520
And if there's any
outlier, we basically

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:56.800
can say, OK, this
atomic clock is bad.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.300
We don't care
about this anymore,

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:03.070
and we use the ones that
are looking more reasonable.

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:06.280
Now, we synchronize
these clocks--

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:07.770
we describe it in our paper--

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:11.020
like roughly every 30 seconds.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.839
And as you can see
there, the clock drift

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:17.500
is about 200
microseconds per second.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:20.710
So the interval that
you can get down

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:24.760
to like single digits, very low
single digits, milliseconds.

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:27.550
So that basically means,
all over the world,

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:30.850
we can get an interval where
we know the lower timestamp

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.640
and the upper timestamp
are not further away

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:36.280
than two milliseconds or
maybe one millisecond.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.660
And we know that the lower
bound has passed everywhere

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:40.930
and the upper bound
has passed nowhere.

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:45.230
And that enables us to do
this global consistency.

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:47.200
Now, some of you might
ask what happened

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:50.620
if all my atomic clocks
fail in the data center

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:53.980
or all my time masters
fail in the data center.

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:56.590
Now, we can still get these
true timestamps or timestamp

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.410
intervals from a
different data center.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:04.150
And we calculate the
time of the transmission

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:05.610
and things like that.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:09.400
What basically means, this
interval becomes bigger.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:12.130
Maybe this interval is
then 10 milliseconds or 100

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:16.160
milliseconds, which basically
means, yes, our database

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:19.570
system is slowing down,
but it's not stopping.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:20.950
We can still operate.

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:22.860
We just slow down a bit.

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:26.360
And that, I think,
is pretty amazing.

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:31.100
Now, let's look at a
more practical example,

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:35.840
and I'd like to explain that
based on a life of a query.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:39.200
So in this case, I am
selecting all events where

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:41.450
the name is Cloud Summit Paris.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:45.740
So think of the agenda
that we get back for this.

00:18:45.740 --> 00:18:47.990
Now, the client sends a request.

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:52.370
And in our case here, it comes
in through one of our followers

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:53.580
in this Paxos group.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.680
So we have a leader in zone
two, and we have two followers

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.030
in zone three and zone one.

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:01.070
Now, the request comes
in through the follower

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:05.870
in zone one, and that one
will basically ask the leader

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:09.520
and say, hey, do I have
the most recent data?

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.980
I am seeing this timestamp of
the data that is requested.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:15.470
Is this the latest
timestamp that you have?

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:18.800
Now, if the leader says
yep, you're current,

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:23.210
I can just serve that data
from a follower and I'm done.

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:27.490
Now, in the case that my request
comes in and my followers--

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:29.750
or my replica, in this case--

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:33.290
doesn't have the
data, the leader

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:35.480
will respond and
say no, you don't

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.340
have the most current data.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:38.450
But just wait.

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:40.580
The data is already in flight.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:43.250
So just wait out that
time till you have

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:45.810
this timestamp of your data.

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:47.720
And once you see
that timestamp, you

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:49.460
can serve the data
because then you'd

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:52.920
know for sure that you
have the most current data.

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.500
So I basically just
wait, and then I

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:57.480
send the response to my client.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:00.960
Now, another really
powerful feature in Spanner

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:06.090
is that you can do
consistent stale reads.

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.470
Now, what does that mean?

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:09.000
A consistent stale
read basically

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.520
means that you
provide a boundary

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:14.835
or you provide a specific
timestamp within seven days

00:20:14.835 --> 00:20:17.730
where you say, I want to see
the state of the database

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.890
on that specific
timestamp, or I want

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:23.850
to see the state of the database
and I don't care if it's up to,

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:25.230
let's say, 15 seconds old.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.000
So basically, in this
case, I'm sending a request

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:30.970
from my client.

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:33.480
It comes in again through
one of the replicas.

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:36.420
And I say, it's fine if
this data is 15 seconds old.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:43.230
In most of the cases--

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.810
like, in most, most,
most, most of the cases--

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:48.217
this data will be
already at the replica,

00:20:48.217 --> 00:20:50.550
and they will have a timestamp
where they know, OK, they

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:53.760
can serve this data which is
within this boundary of 15

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.290
seconds staleness.

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:58.170
And they can just
respond to the client.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:00.850
We don't have to ask
the leader or anything.

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:02.970
And this is especially
powerful if you

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:06.480
have a Cloud Spanner instance
that is globally distributed.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:11.040
That is, you have, let's say,
a read-write master set up

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.470
or read-write quorum
in the US, and then you

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:17.070
have read-through replicas
in Europe and in Asia.

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:19.980
Now, if you have Asian
clients coming in

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.070
and they don't care if they
have the most current data

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:25.650
but they want to have a
consistent state of data,

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:29.270
they can be served with data
immediately from the read

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:31.080
replicas in Asia
without consulting

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:33.330
the read-write quorum in the US.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:36.790
And that speeds up your
queries quite a bit.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:39.760
Now, if we look at the
read-write transaction,

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:41.740
you can imagine in a
read-write transaction

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.790
I need the leader involved.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.230
I'm changing the state.

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:47.610
And I'm globally
consistent, so I

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:50.410
need the leader to be involved.

00:21:50.410 --> 00:21:53.640
So in this case, we are
routing the request directly

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:56.710
to the leader of the Paxos
groups that are involved.

00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:58.580
So if multiple Paxos
groups are involved,

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:01.800
we use two-phase commit
to basically communicate

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:03.270
and synchronize between them.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:05.100
If a single Paxos
group is involved,

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:07.320
we basically can just
do the transaction

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:09.340
within this Paxos group.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:15.490
Now, in my example here,
I want to update my talk

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:16.350
as you can see here.

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:18.960
So the first thing that
I do is I do a read.

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:23.370
I get my query result and
already acquiring some logs.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:25.500
And then I'm doing some changes.

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:29.140
I'm writing this
mutation to a buffer,

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:32.160
and then I'm committing
this transaction.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:34.770
And the leader
basically in that time

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.490
sends out the write
requests of this new data

00:22:37.490 --> 00:22:40.080
to the followers, the replicas.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:43.770
And as soon as a quorum, so the
majority of that Paxos group,

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:47.320
responds with yes, I
have written that data,

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:50.070
we can respond to
the client and say

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:52.300
your transaction
has been successful

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:53.910
and we can release the locks.

00:22:57.090 --> 00:23:02.190
Now, as for the data
format or the layout, what

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:06.990
we have in Spanner
is pretty much

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:09.910
what you're used to from a
traditional database system.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:12.500
So we have tables with columns
that are strongly typed.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:14.610
As you can see
here, as an example

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:19.290
I have a singers table
and an albums table and I

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:20.700
want to join them--

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:22.080
for instance, by
the primary key.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.730
In this case, I'm using the
singer ID as an example.

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:27.870
Now, in traditional
database systems,

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.840
you have something like
a foreign key constraint

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:33.630
where it can say, OK,
if I delete a singer,

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:37.150
I want to have all my
albums deleted as well.

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:38.760
Now, in a distributed
system, this

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:41.800
becomes really complicated
and very harmful,

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:43.950
so we don't support
this semantic.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:47.700
But we support a little bit
like a foreign key constraint

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:49.776
minus minus semantic.

00:23:49.776 --> 00:23:51.900
So in case you have something
where you have tables

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.940
that you join a
lot and where you

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:58.110
want to be sure that this data
in these tables is co-located,

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:03.570
we have a construct that is
called interleaved tables.

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:06.320
So in interleaved tables,
what happens is basically

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:11.000
you interleave your child
table into a parent table

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:13.070
based on the primary key.

00:24:13.070 --> 00:24:16.260
So the child table
has to basically

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:19.850
be precluded with the primary
key of the parent table.

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:23.240
And then we basically can
interleave this data right

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:27.050
where the parent
rows are living.

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:31.910
So basically, what happens is
they end up in the same split.

00:24:31.910 --> 00:24:37.470
And as you know, the split is
managed by one Paxos group,

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.700
so we don't have to go to
different compute nodes

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.350
and get all the
data together if we

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:44.930
do this [INAUDIBLE] every time.

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:47.660
Now, you have to be a
bit careful about this.

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:51.310
Don't overuse interleaving
because, as just mentioned,

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:52.730
it all ends up in one split.

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:55.670
So you can run into
things like hotspotting

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:58.970
or you can exhaust actually
the storage limits of a split.

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:02.130
So you have to be a
bit careful there.

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:05.970
Now, what does it look like in
SQL or in the DDL statement?

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:09.000
You basically just add like
the interleave in [? zingers ?]

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:10.800
or the interleave
in the parent table,

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:13.310
and then you can actually
have multiple levels

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:17.360
of this interleaving
if you need to.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:19.980
Another powerful feature
that I mentioned earlier

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.220
is online schema updates.

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:26.760
So if you want to
evolve your application

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:29.610
and you want to add columns
or things like that,

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:33.100
you can do that while all
your load is going on.

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:34.950
And it is done in a transaction.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.230
So they are transactionally
consistent if you

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:38.580
do a schema update.

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:41.340
For instance, here, if I'm
changing my singers table

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:47.140
and add a column H, I can do
that inside a transaction.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:50.410
So there a couple of schema
design don'ts which are coming

00:25:50.410 --> 00:25:52.510
from the nature of this
being a distributed,

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:54.770
horizontal-scalable database.

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:58.180
So if you come from a
MySQL or Postgres world,

00:25:58.180 --> 00:26:01.360
this is not a lift and
shift that you can do.

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:03.370
You can't take your
application that you

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:08.590
have built on top of MySQL or
Postgres and just move it over.

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:13.410
You have to do some redesigning
and some changes on your schema

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:16.160
in most of the cases.

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:19.330
So one of the things that
are really harmful in Spanner

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:22.870
are things like an outer
increment in Tetra.

00:26:22.870 --> 00:26:26.200
So think of outer
increment feature in MySQL

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.570
where every time I
insert a row it gets

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:32.290
automatically assigned a new ID.

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:37.000
Now, if we have like these, we
have monotonically increasing

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:37.840
numbers.

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:40.450
And as I mentioned earlier,
we are doing splits

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:50.170
by alphabetically-sorted
ranges or lexicographic ranges.

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:53.320
Now, you can imagine, if you
have a monotonically increasing

00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:57.790
ID or timestamps, for instance,
every time you insert data

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:01.404
it basically gets inserted
into your last split.

00:27:01.404 --> 00:27:03.070
And your hotspotting
on your last split.

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:05.710
Even though you might have a
thousand splits for your table

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:09.880
and you always insert row with
the most current timestamp,

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:12.970
you're basically sending all
this data to the last split.

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.400
And by that, you
restrict your scalability

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:18.760
and you hotspot on that split.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:22.810
Again, also, with
interleaving, the same problem.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:25.060
If you interleave a lot
of data into one row,

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:28.060
into one parent row, you
interleave all this data

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:28.810
into one split.

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:30.970
And again, you can
also cause hotspotting.

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:34.210
So be careful of how deep
you go and how much data

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:37.160
you interleave into
a parent table.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:40.130
So just to give an example,
this is a little bit more

00:27:40.130 --> 00:27:41.540
graphically here.

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:44.360
If you have at timestamp
as a primary key,

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:47.030
like a last access
timestamp for instance,

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:50.780
and then user ID and some other
data for each of your rows,

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:53.030
since it's
lexicographically ordered,

00:27:53.030 --> 00:27:56.850
you get all these
into one split.

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:59.460
So what can you do to
avoid this hotspotting?

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:03.840
So one of the things that
you can do is using UIDs--

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:07.040
and they are preferable
Version 4 or later--

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:08.730
as your primary keys.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:12.870
You can do things like you add
a charting counter or charting

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:15.230
ID to your data in front.

00:28:15.230 --> 00:28:18.580
You had a charting idea to your
timestamp and things like that.

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:20.790
So as an example here,
again, same table

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:23.940
as we saw before, we have
our last access timestamp,

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:26.220
we have our user ID, and we
just add in front of it a

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:27.510
sharding ID.

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:30.750
And by adding this sharding
ID, now we can actually

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:34.110
distribute the load
off inserts or updates

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:36.410
on these rows across
multiple splits

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:40.280
and you're not
hotspotting on a split.

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:43.970
So with this, I hope
you got some insights

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:47.420
on how Cloud Spanner
works under the hood

00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:51.030
and what things to
pay attention to.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.110
