00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:37.340
detailed about their transition
from sharded MySQL system

00:29:37.340 --> 00:29:38.296
to Cloud Spanner.

00:29:38.296 --> 00:29:39.920
So if you are in the
situation that you

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:41.570
will have a sharded
system and you

00:29:41.570 --> 00:29:44.090
want to explore
Cloud Spanner, this

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:47.360
is a good start to look
into what are the things

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:49.460
to pay attention to.

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:52.850
Another thing that we
sometimes get asked is,

00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:55.280
does Cloud Spanner
defeat the CAP theory?

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:00.140
Now, if you look at
distributed systems

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:05.470
and you know Eric Brewer, he
came up with this CAP theory

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.530
where you have to
basically choose two out

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:12.450
of three of consistency,
availability, and partitioning.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:19.220
So you can't have a consistent
available system that is also

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:21.700
partitioned, things like that.

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:23.900
And now, I'm asked, OK,
does Spanner actually

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:25.640
defeat the CAP theory?

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:28.000
And the answer is no.

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:30.340
If Cloud Spanner becomes
partitioned to the thing

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:33.820
that we don't have a majority
anymore for our Paxos groups,

00:30:33.820 --> 00:30:39.430
we default to a consistent
system and become unavailable.

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:42.380
But the likelihood is
very, very, very low.

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:44.610
Otherwise, we wouldn't
give an SLA of four nines

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.290
for regional, five
nines for multiregional.

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:49.720
Because of the system
and the hardware that we

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:53.650
have underneath and the way
the Spanner was architectured,

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:56.650
this likelihood
of a split brain,

00:30:56.650 --> 00:31:01.062
how it's called sometimes,
is very, very low.

00:31:01.062 --> 00:31:05.280
Now, another thing that
I want to point out is,

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:08.990
if you look into building a
new application or migrating

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:13.170
an application, please read
our white papers that we have

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:16.380
linked in the documentation
which talks a lot about

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:19.230
do's and don'ts in terms of
schema design and creative

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:20.070
design.

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:21.990
There are some specifics
to Cloud Spanner

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:23.385
to get the most
out of it, and we

00:31:23.385 --> 00:31:25.260
want to enable you to
get the most out of it.

