00:36:48.030 --> 00:36:51.240
Now, in case you need to
authenticate with a key,

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:55.050
we also have the possibility
that you create a JSON

00:36:55.050 --> 00:36:58.320
key like a service
account, and then

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:01.620
you use that to authenticate
against Cloud Spanner.

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:05.010
Now, the next thing is
I need a client start.

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:08.790
So I need to say which
instance am I talking to,

00:37:08.790 --> 00:37:13.020
which database am I talking to,
and use all this information.

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:14.880
The project ID and
things like that come

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:17.670
from these
authentication contexts.

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:20.250
And then, I create
a database client.

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:22.550
So as you can see here,
I'm creating my client.

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:27.360
Next thing, what I want
to do is, in this case,

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:28.860
a read-only transaction.

00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:31.050
So a read-only transaction,
the advantage of this

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:33.630
is I don't need to
acquire any logs.

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:35.820
So I can just do this.

00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:40.220
We don't need protection.

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:42.980
And basically, what
you do is DB line

00:37:42.980 --> 00:37:46.440
dot single use for your
read-only transaction

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:47.970
in our case.

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:51.270
And then, I want to,
of course, run a query.

00:37:51.270 --> 00:37:53.190
So here, in this
case, I am looking

00:37:53.190 --> 00:37:57.660
for all my monthly events that
are happening from right now

00:37:57.660 --> 00:38:00.820
till tomorrow, so
24 hours later.

00:38:00.820 --> 00:38:04.060
So as you can see here, I'm
using a statement builder,

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:08.730
and I can put
parameter placeholders

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:12.790
and then bind these parameters
down here as you can see.

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:15.060
So I'm using, basically,
for the date timestamp now,

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:19.290
and then for the next day I
am creating a new timestamp

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:23.420
where I'm adding 24 hours.

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:25.290
And last but not
least, I, of course,

00:38:25.290 --> 00:38:29.780
want to show this and iterate
through my result set.

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:33.600
So I basically just, as
we know from iterable,

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:35.730
we just iterate
through the list.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:38.210
We don't have any
results that's left.

00:38:38.210 --> 00:38:41.990
And I can point to the
results in from my query

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:44.480
either by locations or ID.

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:49.160
Like you can see, getString
I0, which basically means

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:51.530
I want to have the first
position of my query,

00:38:51.530 --> 00:38:56.240
or you can also use the
column names if you use them.

00:38:56.240 --> 00:39:00.080
So I could use, basically,
multi-event ID, for instance,

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:05.640
to get the value of this for
this row for this column.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:08.770
And then I can run all this.

00:39:08.770 --> 00:39:11.810
And fingers crossed,
it hopefully works.

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:13.780
So see, I also limited
it to 10 results.

00:39:13.780 --> 00:39:15.880
So we see that it runs.

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:18.310
Now, this took a
little bit, and that

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:21.460
is because creating the
connection to Cloud Spanner

00:39:21.460 --> 00:39:22.840
takes a little bit of time.

00:39:22.840 --> 00:39:26.960
If I would run it multiple times
with the connection standing,

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:28.150
it gets much, much faster.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:32.380
So you get to single-digit
query response times actually.

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:37.510
Now, I mentioned that we can
do is stale consistent reads.

00:39:37.510 --> 00:39:39.580
And what I can do
is I just modify

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:43.990
my single read-only transaction,
and I'm adding a time bound.

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:47.830
So as you can see up there, I
am adding at timestamp bound off

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:51.220
a max staleness of 15 seconds.

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:52.930
And by that,
basically, if I come

00:39:52.930 --> 00:39:55.490
in through a follower
or a replica,

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:58.630
the replica can respond
with the data right away.

00:39:58.630 --> 00:40:00.685
So we can just run
this again, and you

00:40:00.685 --> 00:40:04.996
will see that you get
some data back as well.

00:40:04.996 --> 00:40:06.620
Now, the next thing
that I want to show

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:08.540
is a read-only transaction.

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:12.020
Now, I have all this
stuff here already,

00:40:12.020 --> 00:40:15.140
so this is just a repetition.

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:19.460
So I'm going to jump right
away to the insert transaction.

00:40:19.460 --> 00:40:22.770
Now, what I do here is a
write-only transaction.

00:40:22.770 --> 00:40:25.130
And first of all, what I
have to create is a mutation.

00:40:25.130 --> 00:40:27.620
So we don't have currently DML.

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:32.060
You can't do insert into
and things like that.

00:40:32.060 --> 00:40:34.670
But you can use the
client libraries,

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:38.020
and they have an automatic
way of inserting data.

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:41.010
Now, there's something that we
use that's called mutations,

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:44.080
and I want to elaborate
a little bit on this.

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:47.990
In Spanner, a mutation is
basically a change of a cell,

00:40:47.990 --> 00:40:49.570
like a modification of a cell.

00:40:49.570 --> 00:40:52.580
So that means if you have
a table with five columns

00:40:52.580 --> 00:40:56.840
and you insert a row,
you have five mutations.

00:40:56.840 --> 00:40:58.720
Now, why do I mention that?

00:40:58.720 --> 00:41:00.330
The thing is, for
transaction, we

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:03.730
have a limit of 20,000
mutations per transaction.

00:41:03.730 --> 00:41:07.420
And you have to be aware of
this if you are adding indexes

00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:08.620
on your table.

00:41:08.620 --> 00:41:11.200
For instance, we have
our five-column table

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:14.280
and we add an index
with three columns

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:17.770
where we do an index on
three of the five columns.

00:41:17.770 --> 00:41:21.550
Then we basically have, for each
row that we add in our table,

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:24.820
we have eight mutations,
five for the table

00:41:24.820 --> 00:41:27.160
and three for the index.

00:41:27.160 --> 00:41:29.250
And it's really,
really important

00:41:29.250 --> 00:41:33.340
to remember if you ever see
that error exceeding mutations

00:41:33.340 --> 00:41:36.790
limit, that you think
OK, a mutation is not

00:41:36.790 --> 00:41:38.190
one row, one mutation.

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:44.350
A mutation is one row multiplied
by columns and so on and so on.

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:47.760
So in this case, I'm
creating an account.

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:52.060
I'm adding my name, email
address and everything.

00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:54.700
And then I run this.

00:41:58.010 --> 00:42:02.200
There's one thing that
I want to mention.

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:07.550
Basically Here, you see I'm
basically doing DBClient.write

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:09.410
and I provide these mutations.

00:42:09.410 --> 00:42:11.300
And I am just writing that.

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:13.630
So this will return
me the timestamp

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:15.660
when this has been written.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:18.840
Now, we have this
in the database,

00:42:18.840 --> 00:42:22.340
and I can show
that really quick.

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:25.460
I have here my demo
instance, and I'm

00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:26.870
going to go to the accounts.

00:42:26.870 --> 00:42:28.700
And if you go to
Data, we're going

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:32.520
to see here my data
that's just got inserted.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:35.500
Now, one of the things
that I want to do is,

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:39.450
if I want to do a read-write, so
I have to create a transaction

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:43.440
context in my client,
and then do the read,

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:48.120
which I can do either through
the read API or I can use SQL--

00:42:48.120 --> 00:42:52.880
so in this case, I'm
creating this read here--

00:42:52.880 --> 00:42:54.640
and then my mutation.

00:42:54.640 --> 00:42:57.020
But there's something
special about this construct.

00:42:57.020 --> 00:42:59.860
As you can see here, we do
this read-write transaction

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:04.250
and we provide a transaction
runner, transaction callable.

00:43:04.250 --> 00:43:05.850
Now, why are we doing this?

00:43:05.850 --> 00:43:07.910
As you can see
here, we basically

00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:11.690
provide the client
library complete

00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:13.940
function of this transaction.

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:15.890
And that enables us
from the client side

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:19.370
to rerun your transaction
in case it fails.

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:21.140
So you basically
provide us all of what

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:22.880
has to be done in
this transaction,

00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:24.910
and we can rerun it
on the client side

00:43:24.910 --> 00:43:28.400
if there should be any
problems with this.

00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:31.790
So again, if we run this,
I'm just basically selecting

00:43:31.790 --> 00:43:35.030
my account and I'm changing
my name from Robert

00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:36.770
to Spanner Guru.

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:44.350
So if we go back here and go to
our account table, to my data,

00:43:44.350 --> 00:43:47.860
we now see that my
name has changed.

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:51.460
And then, I could do this
also programmatically,

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:55.380
but we just recently added this
feature in our Cloud Console

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:58.740
that you can now also edit
data right here or delete it.

00:43:58.740 --> 00:44:01.670
In my case, I want to
clean up after myself,

00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:03.750
so I delete this data.

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:05.230
Let's switch back to the slides.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:15.030
So with one minute
left, I just want

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:18.210
to mention briefly
the other database

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:21.870
products that we have in our
Cloud Platform portfolio.

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:25.440
So our database products
range from in-memory databases

00:44:25.440 --> 00:44:28.290
to the data warehouse databases.

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:30.420
So if you, for instance,
build an App Engine,

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:33.870
you can use our App
Engine Memcache.

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:37.360
If you look at a relational, we
have managed database servers

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:40.380
where you can have MySQL
or Postgres as a managed

00:44:40.380 --> 00:44:42.660
solution, which can be
set up with failover

00:44:42.660 --> 00:44:44.320
and read replicas.

00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:46.680
But that is a
vertical-scalable solution.

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:48.990
Now, if you need
horizontal-scalable solutions

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:52.830
for the criteria that I
mentioned throughout this talk,

00:44:52.830 --> 00:44:54.960
you are looking
at Cloud Spanner.

00:44:54.960 --> 00:44:59.700
If you look at more of
the NoSQL databases,

00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:01.650
on the one hand Cloud
Datastore, which

00:45:01.650 --> 00:45:04.110
is more like a document store.

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:05.820
And then we have
Cloud Bigtable, which

00:45:05.820 --> 00:45:11.230
has key values which can
have immense throughput

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:13.740
and scale with your needs
on the throughput side

00:45:13.740 --> 00:45:15.210
and also on the
query side in terms

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:17.550
of single-digit
query response times

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:20.640
even if you have terabytes
of data in your database.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.470
Now, Cloud Datastore
supports you

00:45:22.470 --> 00:45:25.860
from small applications
up to huge applications.

00:45:25.860 --> 00:45:29.220
Just think, Snapchat built
their entire application

00:45:29.220 --> 00:45:32.280
on Datastore and App Engine.

00:45:32.280 --> 00:45:35.190
And then, if you look more
onto object solutions,

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:36.790
you can look into
Cloud Storage where

00:45:36.790 --> 00:45:39.975
you can save basically
blobs, like video files.

00:45:42.594 --> 00:45:44.010
The nice thing
about cloud storage

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:46.710
is also it's backed
by Spanner again.

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:49.530
And it enables you to,
if you store a file

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:51.050
and you look
anywhere in the world

00:45:51.050 --> 00:45:54.390
on the state of what kind of
files you have in your buckets,

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:57.190
you will see a consistent
state across the globe.

00:45:57.190 --> 00:46:00.480
So if your data is there, you
will see it from everywhere.

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:02.610
And that's enabled
by Cloud Spanner.

00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:04.630
Now, if you want
to do analytics,

00:46:04.630 --> 00:46:09.060
we have a Cloud-native
solution, fully-managed solution

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:12.450
called BigQuery, and
BigQuery is amazing to crunch

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:14.520
through terabytes
even petabytes of data

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:17.410
in seconds or very low minutes.

00:46:17.410 --> 00:46:21.930
So if you're looking at
lowering your costs of ownership

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:26.100
in doing analytics, BigQuery
is one of your things

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:27.570
where you should look into.

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:29.530
With this, thank you very much.

00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:33.300
I hope you enjoyed this talk,
and enjoy the rest of the day.

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:35.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:37.250
